hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in his work fear and trembling sir and  Kierkegaard introduces a certain  character or if you want to be a little  bit more exact a human type a  possibility as he says for for all of us  if we so choose to embrace and endorse  that type he calls it the night of  infinite resignation and it's a very  good thing to be such a night  the word night and the associations with  chivalry should not mislead us into  thinking that he's only talking about  men this is equally possible for women  as some of the examples that he will use  show us but there is an idea about  recognizing some sort of motivational  value that that centers one's life that  you could even express in a vow so the  the courtly or chivalric notion here is  definitely on point I should also point  out that there is another type of night  a yet higher sort that he will compare  and contrast the night of infinite  resignation against and that is the  night of faith who will talk about  elsewhere now most people are neither of  these nights most people are actually  lost in what Kierkegaard calls the  aesthetic he doesn't talk about that  quite so much in this work but  Kierkegaard has this notion of three  basic stages or perspectives or outlooks  on life there's the aesthetic where we  are concerned with the finite  particulars that were engaged in we are  spreading ourselves out over many  different things there is no single  concentration giving our life a center  and value we absorb things over and over  again and we may weigh them against each  other but really any of them are  exchangeable for for any others the  ethical  is different when we move from the  aesthetic into the ethical we recognize  that there are what we might call  definite and fixed hierarchies of goods  there are prioritizations that we want  to make or at least recognize if we fail  to see them through in actual practice  and within the ethical there can be a  number of different let's call them  smaller stages but there is some  recognition at each of those stages that  there's some value that trumps  everything else and the night of  infinite resignation is somebody who  moves within the ethical that doesn't  mean that the aesthetic doesn't exist  for them but the ethical centers and  Orient's everything Kierkegaard has a  wonderful line here that we're going to  come back to when we talk about the leap  into the infinite he talks about most  people living completely absorbed in  worldly joys and sorrows he calls them  Benchwarmers who do not take part in the  dance he tells us that the nights of  infinity are ballet dancers and have  elevation they leap up into the infinite  and we'll talk about precisely what that  means towards the end of this little  discussion first however let's talk  about this other aspect Kierkegaard  tells us that the night of infinite  resignation in his own words will have  the power to concentrate the as he says  the whole substance of his life and the  meaning of actuality into one single  desire notice those two things the  meaning of his or her life what it is  that they choose to make their life be  about in the whole of actuality going  beyond merely themselves and thinking  about what gives the world its value  what makes things important this is  something distinctively human that we  can do we are meaning producing  creatures  and this is a very important aspect of  how in fact we we do behave so  concentrating one's life and meaning  into one desire another way of putting  this could be one value one thing that  we see as inherently valuable that we  would not exchange for something else or  sacrifice to other things  we might even talk about it as one love  and Kierkegaard will use one's love as  an example of this but we could also  think more broadly in terms of whatever  it is that occupies our heart and has  its call upon us that we recognize and  choose to heed he tells us as well that  the night of Infinite resignation will  resist very important temptation a  temptation to make themselves either to  forget who they are now why might they  do this well the night of infinite  resignation not only recognizes some  value as intrinsically higher than the  others but has to resign him or herself  precisely because that value cannot in  this finite world be realized in the way  that they would like to see it happen so  one way of dealing with that is to  forget who you are and become somebody  different and no longer be motivated in  that way people who choose to try to  lose their themselves in drugs or  alcohol or some other addictive thing  you know lose themselves an exercise  lose themselves in dieting lose  themselves in work in making money in  having one affair after another after  another they're not going to be the  knight of infinite resignation and  they're precluding that possibility for  themselves Kierkegaard says will this  person forget it all no Annie  though that might be a sort of  concentration why he says the night does  not contradict himself and it's a  contradiction to forget the whole  substance of his life and yet remain the  same he feels no inclination to become  another person by no means regards that  is something great  only the lower nature's Kierkegaard says  forget themselves and become something  new a little bit later he says only the  lower nature's have the law for their  actions in someone else the premises for  their actions outside of themselves  so once you've attained to this point  where you're you can recognize and say  this is absolutely important this is  absolutely valuable I ought to orient  myself to it even though I'm not going  to be successful even though the world  is against me in this you don't abandon  that you don't abandon the value and you  don't abandon yourself in the process  again for Kierkegaard this is showing us  almost the very best of what human  beings are capable of the night of  infinite resignation does engage in  resignation and resignation is not  simply a moping around and saying oh I  can't hear of what I want it's not a  form of self-pity it is an acceptance of  how things are and a recognition that  they really could be otherwise but won't  be otherwise that they should be  otherwise that the value should be  realizable and yet won't be realizable  they resign themselves to what we can  call the impossibility within this  finite world of whatever it is that they  are valuing and Kierkegaard talks about  this in terms of here we go  he says fools and young people say  everything is possible for a human being  fools do because they don't know any  better that's what makes them fools  young people also say that because  they're rather hopeful they haven't had  enough  disappointments at least many young  people there are many young people who  have been so brutally disappointed or  brutalized and their horizons have  shortened incredibly but many of them  say everything is possible  Kierkegaard says that's a mistake  why on one side everything is possible  on another side it's not the case  he says spiritually speaking everything  is possible within the realm of the  infinite but in the finite world there  is much that is not possible we have for  example bodies that we don't entirely  control and we can do many things to  them you want to lose weight you can  diet and exercise and do all sorts of  other things as well which may or may  not work for you but you can't keep your  body from eventually breaking down  through aging maybe we'll even have  technology to reverse aging but sooner  or later something is going to catch up  with you and if you want that technology  it's very expensive so you'd better make  a lot of money but you might not be able  to make a lot of money in this finite  world there may be nobody who wants to  pay what you would like for your talents  so that you can realize this other good  you don't want to be spending all your  time hustling and making money you want  to attain this value over here but it's  not possible Kierkegaard uses other  examples of for example you know an  unhappy love unhappy in the sense that  it cannot be realized  two people are deeply in love with each  other deeply in love not just in the  sense of infatuation but appreciating  who the other person genuinely is and  wanting to live in a full lifetime  shared life relationship with that  person and yet it's simply not possible  given the finite circumstances that one  lives in we could take patriotism as an  example as well when patriotism is not  just jingoism and waving the flag but a  genuine law  of one's own country and the people who  people's many times who inhabit it that  can actually recognize the goodness of  other countries it can be an incredibly  potent value people will lay down their  lives for that sort of thing and rightly  so but it could be that your country  will still be conquered or that it'll be  run by some cabal of terrible people or  that the very patriotism that you  espouse will be used by those who just  want to engage in Wars or conflicts with  other people you don't control that so  resigning oneself to this this  impossibility is part of what the knight  is doing but notice what else he says  the knight makes the impossibility  possible by expressing it spiritually in  the realm that we can in fact have some  control over but he expresses it  spiritually by renouncing it he says the  desire that would lead him out into  actuality but as stranded on  impossibility is now turned inward this  is another aspect of us human being the  the common human nature that we share  that we can in fact turn inward we can  have an interior life even if everybody  else is against me and tells me that I'm  crazy for pursuing desiring loving this  this valuable thing I can still keep it  within myself and choose to protect it  protect that love protect that desire  from that hostile world but it means  that that's the only place that it  exists and in many cases there is a sort  of compensation for this Kierkegaard  points out he talks about the resilience  of resignation a capacity to continue on  to persevere he also talks about  becoming self-sufficient he says that  the one who has resigned in  not just finitely but infinitely is  sufficient to oneself the night does not  cancel his resignation it keeps his  loved just as young as it was in the  first moment this is about somebody  who's in love with somebody and cannot  have that relationship he never loses it  simply because he has made the movement  infinitely what the princess does cannot  disturb him only again only the lower  nature's have their law for their  actions in someone else the premises for  their actions outside themselves he says  in Infinite resignation we can find a  certain peace and rest he says every  person who wills it who is not to based  himself by self disdain can just  discipline himself to make this movement  this is a possibility for you and for me  for any of us who move beyond the  captivation of the world and the  esthetic into the ethical he says in  this we experience some pain but this  pain reconciles us to existence and he  says there's also a comfort in the pain  one can in fact do this this is a  genuine possibility this is not the best  thing for Kierkegaard but it's pretty  pretty good it's far better than most  other ways of living the last thing that  I want to point out I mentioned that the  night of infinite resignation exists in  relation to yet another night who  surpasses this one in certain ways both  of them to use this metaphor of the  ballet dancer leap into the infinite  they leap into something higher  something Universal and then they come  back down to the finite world and  Kierkegaard has this wonderful analogy  here where he he says that both of them  are making the movements of infinity all  right but the night of faith is doing it  somewhat different than the night of  finitude is doing so he says it is  supposed to be the most difficult feat  for a ballet dancer to leap into a  specific posture in such a way he never  wants strains for the posture but in the  very leap assumes the posture perhaps  there is no ballet dancer who can do it  but this night does it he says the  nights of infinity our ballet dancers  and have elevation they make the upward  movement and come down again and this  too is not an unhappy diversion and not  unlovely to see but here's the  difference he says every time they come  down they are unable to assume the  posture immediately they waver for a  moment and this wavering shows they are  aliens in the world it is more or less  conspicuous according to their skill but  even the most skillful of these nights  cannot hide this wavering which means  that like Kierkegaard said they are in  fact aliens or sojourners or whatever  term you prefer to say within this  finite world and yet they are in a  certain respect the greatest hope for  realizing genuine values within this  world each of them is a sort of world on  to him or herself which is why there is  a self-sufficiency there so this is a  very positive c